@ Index:   Propositional logic: tautology, contradiction or contingency, and the truth table
@ Author:  Victor Valdes
@ Version: 0.2.0
@ Source:  https://github.com/valdesvj/logic47
@ Tested:  on the C47 by the author (Oct 2026)
@ Tested:  C47 simulator (python/c47sim.py): tests/test_logic47.py, 39 fixed formulas and 317 random ones of
@ Tested:  1-6 variables against a separate Python evaluator
@ Input:   the formula, keyed with F1-F6 on three pages (1 p q r s t u, 2 the connectives, 3 ( ) T F)
@ Output:  p->q (keys F1, 2 F4, 1 F2, 5): CONTINGENCY, true in 3 of 4 rows, T: p=0 q=0, F: p=1 q=0;
@ Output:  then 6: the table p q | Φ: 1 1 | 1, 1 0 | 0, 0 1 | 1, 0 0 | 1 (Φ the formula's value)
@
@ ---- What it is ----
@ Logic 47 checks a formula of propositional logic. You type a formula such as p -> (q v r) with the soft keys and
@ the C47 tells you whether it is a TAUTOLOGY (always true), a CONTRADICTION (always false) or a CONTINGENCY
@ (sometimes true), in how many rows of the truth table it is true, and gives one row where it is true and one
@ where it is false. Key 6 then shows the whole truth table. Each variable is held as a 64-bit word, so the
@ calculator evaluates all 64 rows at once with AND, OR, XOR and NOT: the answer comes in well under a second.
@
@ ---- How to use it ----
@ Load LOGIC47 and press XEQ LOGIC47. The soft keys F1-F6 type the symbols of the page shown under the screen:
@   1         page 1: the variables p q r s t u
@   2         page 2: the connectives  not  and  or  implies  if-and-only-if  exclusive-or
@   3         page 3: the brackets ( ), true (shown T) and false
@   4         delete the last symbol            7   clear the formula
@   5         evaluate: TAUTOLOGY, CONTRADICTION or CONTINGENCY, the true rows, and an example row of each kind;
@             a formula that is not well formed gives SYNTAX ERROR
@   6         after 5: the truth table (up to 64 rows on one screen, in blocks of 16); any key goes back
@   0         the end
@ Precedence, from the highest: not, and, or / exclusive-or, implies, if-and-only-if. So p v q ^ r is p v (q ^ r).
@ "implies" groups to the right (p -> q -> r is p -> (q -> r)); the others to the left. Use brackets when in doubt.
@ Example: p -> q (F1; 2 then F4; 1 then F2; 5) gives CONTINGENCY, true in 3 of 4 rows, T: p=0 q=0, F: p=1 q=0.
@
@ ---- What it changes on your calculator ----
@ Your stack and LASTx are given back at the end. Registers R00-R19 are not touched; R20-R96 are used and cleared
@ at the end. WSIZE is set to 64 (the program needs 64-bit words). No variable, matrix or flag is left behind.
@
@ ---- Programs and labels ----
@ One program, LOGIC47, with one global label (LOGIC47). The routines inside are local labels only (LBL 01-99;
@ LBL 68-73 are the connectives, called with XEQ IND). It uses the STRUCT commands (DO / WHILE / ENDDO), ATEXT,
@ GRFNT and the bitwise commands AND OR XOR NOT BS? BC? of the C47 / R47 firmware.
@
@ ---- Technical notes (from the commented source) ----
@ LOGIC47 v0.2.0 - Logic 47: propositional logic on the C47 / R47
@ A formula of up to six variables p q r s t u, the constants ⊤ ⊥ (drawn T ⊥), the connectives ¬ ∧ ∨ ⊕ (drawn ⊻) → ↔, brackets,
@ keyed with the soft keys F1-F6 (three pages: 1 the variables, 2 the connectives, 3 the brackets and
@ constants). 5 evaluates it: TAUTOLOGY, CONTRADICTION or CONTINGENCY, the number of rows where it is
@ true, and for a contingency a row where it is true and one where it is false.
@ The whole truth table at once: each variable is a 64-bit word, bit k = its value in row k (p = ...1010,
@ q = ...1100, r = ...11110000, ... u = 32 ones then 32 zeros), so the formula evaluated once with AND, OR,
@ XOR and NOT gives the word of its 64 rows. A variable not in the formula only repeats rows: the rows are
@ 2^n for the n variables used, and the true rows the set bits / 2^(6 - n).
@ Precedence, high to low: ¬, ∧, ∨ and ⊕, →, ↔ (→ to the right: p→q→r = p→(q→r); the others to the left).
@ Keys: F1-F6 a symbol of the page, 1 2 3 the page, 4 delete the last symbol, 5 evaluate, 7 clear, 0 end.
@ The user's stack and LASTx are kept; at the end R20-R96 are cleared, WSIZE stays 64.
@ Screen 400 x 240, row 0 = bottom. ATEXT in GRFNT 21 (GRFNT 10 for the small text).
@ Registers: R20 the page, R21 the formula (">" then its symbols), R22 its number of symbols, R23 the key,
@ R24 the result word, R25-R32 the words of p q r s t u ⊤ ⊥ (R25 + the code), R33 the symbol being read,
@ R34 the text, R35 the value stack pointer (values R50-R69), R36 the operator stack pointer (R70-R89),
@ R37 1: syntax error, R38 the code, R39-R45 work, R46 the code letters "abcdefghijklmnop" (code = place),
@ R47 the true rows, R48 a true row, R49 a false row, R90-R92 the pages' code letters, R93 the variables'
@ names, R94-R95 the formula's glyphs, R96 1: a result to show as a table (key 6). R21 holds ">" and the code
@ letters (ASCII), not the glyphs: on the C47 a glyph such as ¬ may take two bytes, and αPOS, αSL, αLEFT would
@ then not count it as one character.
@ Control flow in the C47 STRUCT commands (IF / ELSE / ENDIF, DO / WHILE / ENDDO), indented two spaces per
@ level; tools/build_logic47.py checks them and numbers them.
@

LBL "LOGIC47"
@ the user's stack in the local registers: STOS R.00 (R.00-R.07, the 8 levels), then LASTx in R.08
LocR 9
STOS R.00
LASTx
STO R.08
XEQ 50                          ; -> :INIT:
